# Plan for `Work.EKLC6Core.HcondSig_le_ofReal_haar_uij` (Pisa 7.9 / 7.55, convexity bound)

This file contains NO declarations; it is the written proof plan handed to the prover of
`HcondSig_le_ofReal_haar_uij`.

## Statement

For `U = Uij hij` (`i ≠ j`, `t i < t j`), `T = (aDiag t ·)`, `μ` a `T`-invariant probability
measure on `X₃ = SL₃(ℝ)/SL₃(ℤ)`, `(Z, A)` a *conull* `aDiag t`-descending plaque algebra for `U`
(`IsPlaqueAlgebra U Z A`, `μ Zᶜ = 0`, `comap T A ≤ A`), we must show

    HcondSig μ A (comap T A) ≤ ENNReal.ofReal (t j - t i)          (★)

## Where (★) comes from: Pisa 7.55

`H_μ(A | a⁻¹A) ≤ J := -log det (Ad_{aDiag t} | Lie U) = t j - t i` is proved by the *convexity of
`log`*, as follows.  Write `B := comap T A`.  For `μ`-a.e. `x` let `C x := [x]_B` be the `B`-atom and
`W x := shape B U x = {u ∈ U : u • x ∈ [x]_B}` (a bounded open plaque in `U ≅ ℝ`, `u_ij(s) = 1 + s E_ij`).

1. **Conditional measure = ν-ratio.** By the `condexp` field of `IsLeafwise` applied to the plaque
   algebra `(T⁻¹Z, B)` (see `Work.EKLC6Core.isPlaqueAlgebra_betaAlg`, the file
   `Submission/Work/EKLC6Core/HcondSigFree/HcondSig_le_lintegral_atomInfo_mp.lean` does exactly this
   for `Uminus t`), for every finite partition `P` of `X₃` with `partSigma P ≤ A` one has, for a.e. `x`,

       E[1_{P_i} | B](x) = (ν x (D_i x)) / (ν x (W x)),   D_i x := {u ∈ W x : u • x ∈ P_i}.

2. **Haar comparison (Gibbs).**  Both the σ_i(x) := ν x (D_i x)/ν x (W x) and the Haar weights
   τ_i(x) := m(D_i x)/m(W x) (m = a Haar measure on `U ≅ ℝ`, Lebesgue) are probability weights on the
   same finite partition of `W x`, so `Σ_i σ_i log(τ_i/σ_i) ≤ log (Σ_i τ_i) = 0` (Jensen with the
   concave `log`; the pieces cover `W x` because the `P_i` cover `X₃`).  Hence

       H_μ(P | B) = ∫ Σ_i -σ_i log σ_i dμ ≤ ∫ ( log (m (W x)) - Σ_i σ_i log (m (D_i x)) ) dμ. (∗)

3. **The right-hand side is `J`.**  For `y ∈ C x` one has `m(D_{i(y)}(x)) = m(shape A U y)` (the piece
   is a right translate of the shape: unimodularity of `U ≅ ℝ`), `m(W y) = m(W x)`, and
   `shape A U y` is constant on `A`-atoms; so the second term of (∗) is the conditional expectation
   `∫ x, h(x) dμ` of `h y := -log (m (shape A U y))` and by the tower property of conditional
   expectations (`MeasureTheory.integral_condExp`) it equals `∫ y, -log (m (shape A U y)) dμ`.
   For the first term use `shape B U x = a⁻¹ (shape A U (T x)) a` (this is
   `Submission/Work/EKLC6Core/shape_comap_aDiag.lean`) and (Pisa 7.55a)

       m (a⁻¹ E a) = e^{J} m E   (E ⊆ U),        J = t j - t i,

   which for `U = Uij` is the change of variables `s ↦ e^{J} s` of Lebesgue measure on `ℝ`.  So
   `log (m (W x)) = J + log (m (shape A U (T x)))`, and the two `∫ log (m (shape A U ·))` terms cancel
   by `MeasureTheory.MeasurePreserving.integral_comp` (`μ` is `T`-invariant).

   Result: `H_μ(P | B) ≤ J` for every `P` with `partSigma P ≤ A`, hence `HcondSig μ A (comap T A) ≤ ofReal J`
   (take `iSup_le` over `P`, using `HcondSig = ⨆ P, ofReal (Hcond P (comap T A))`).

4. **The `ℝ≥0∞` bookkeeping (the only genuinely delicate point).**  Steps 1–3 are a chain of *inequalities
   of extended real numbers*; the cancellation in step 3 must not be performed as
   `∫ (log m W) - ∫ log m (shape A U ·)` (both integrals may be `-∞`).  The safe route, which keeps
   everything non-negative, is the following standard truncation:
   * Fix `K` with `log (m (shape A U y)) ≤ K` for `y ∈ Z` (shapes are bounded plaques; for the
     algebras generated by a finite partition of bounded diameter this is uniform; in general take
     `K := log (m (Box1 U))`, which is legal since `V_y ⊆ W_y = a⁻¹ V_{Ty} a` and the volume of `Box1 U`
     is finite — if the shapes are not a priori inside `Box1 U`, use the entry bound of `IsPlaqueAlgebra`).
   * Put `F := K - log (m (shape A U ·)) ≥ 0` (a.e.), `H := E[F | B] ≥ 0`.
   * Then `log m (W x) - h(x) = J + H(x) - F (T x)`, and the pointwise bound of step 2 reads
     `Σ_i -σ_i log σ_i ≤ J + H(x) - F (T x)`, whose right-hand side is `≥ 0` a.e. (the left-hand side is).
   * `∫⁻ x, ENNReal.ofReal (J + H x - F (T x)) ∂μ = ∫⁻ (J + H) - ∫⁻ (F ∘ T)`, because the function is
     `≥ 0` a.e. and both `H` and `F ∘ T` are `ℝ≥0∞`-valued: use `MeasureTheory.lintegral_sub` with
     `∫⁻ (J + H) = J + ∫⁻ H = J + ∫⁻ F = J + ∫⁻ (F ∘ T) < ⊤` — the middle identity is
     `MeasureTheory.lintegral_condExp` (tower, valid for `ℝ≥0∞`-valued functions, no integrability!) and
     the last is `MeasureTheory.MeasurePreserving.lintegral_comp`.  This gives exactly `J`.
   (If `∫⁻ F = ⊤`, apply the argument to `min (F, N)` and pass to the limit; the pointwise Jensen bound
   with `F` replaced by `min (F, N)` holds on the set where the shape masses are `≥ e^{-N + K}`, and
   `N → ∞` is handled by monotone convergence.  Add this if the direct route sticks.)

## References in the workspace

* `Submission/Work/EKLC6Core/HcondSigFree/HcondSig_le_lintegral_atomInfo_mp.lean` — the same proof
  structure for `Uminus t`, with the partition/Gibbs steps 1–2 fully formalised
